= Anchoring effect =

== Overview ==
The '''anchoring effect''' is the influence of an initial value, estimate, or reference point on a later judgment.

Final estimates often remain shifted toward the anchor even when the anchor is weakly informative or arbitrary.

== Classic paradigm ==
Participants may first consider whether a quantity is above or below an anchor and then estimate the quantity.

Higher anchors tend to produce higher later estimates than lower anchors.

This shows that judgments can depend on the starting point used during estimation.

== Not one mechanism ==
Early accounts emphasized insufficient adjustment away from an initial anchor.

Later research also proposes selective accessibility, scale interpretation, conversational inference, numeric priming, and other mechanisms.

Therefore:

<pre>
anchoring effect ≠ insufficient adjustment by definition
</pre>

== Informative and arbitrary anchors ==
Real judgments often begin from useful reference points such as previous prices, forecasts, or historical values.

Experimental research also studies arbitrary anchors to isolate causal influence.

These cases can share behavioral effects while relying on different knowledge.

== Expertise ==
Expertise can reduce some anchoring effects but does not eliminate them universally.

Anchors can influence professional judgment when uncertainty remains and the anchor enters the reasoning process.

== References ==
* Tversky & Kahneman, “Judgment under Uncertainty” (1974).
* anchoring and selective-accessibility research.
* judgment-and-decision reviews.

